A||N||10||2xsess||Your first Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||2xsess||11-21-2000||07:43 AM||||Ok, I see a lot of new members telling stories of their first Fiero purchase. I think it would be cool to hear about everyone's first Fiero.<P>I found my first Fiero in the newspaper in 1992, private seller. It was a red 84 base model. No power anything, just A/C and sunroof. And it had the steel wheels on it. It was advertised for $2000obo. oh and it only had like 60,000 miles on it. I bugged my Mom to take me to go look at it. I called teh guy and made arraingments to go look at it. We drove down and looked at it, I fell in love. Gray interior 4spd. I talked the guy down to $1500. I had to get the money from my account on the following monday but the guy would be out of town until the following weekend. I couldn't hardly contain myself the whole week, it made driving the primer gray 4dr Tempo that I had evne more unbearable. Finally the guy called and said that he'd be home on Saturday but, only for like 3 hours. My best friend and I jumped in my Tempo, the drivers seat finally broke and the top half fell back onto the rear seat, my friend said keep driving I'll prop it up with this ice scraper.lol. We flew down the highway, amazing how people get out of the way of a Tempo doing like 90mph  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I got the car and the first thing I did was get a set of the Tech wheels. A few month later I found a Gt spoiler that someone had for $50, I had it painted black and put it on. I miss that car, full of found memories  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks for reading and sharing(I hope)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sorry it's so long<BR>Jeff||||reg||1
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||11-21-2000||07:59 AM||||   1984 black/gray interior, auto. My ex- wife owned it when we met, her dad was a GM engineer on the Fiero project. It was a fun car but lots of trouble. In 1988 I tried to convince her to trade it for an 88GT, she wanted nothing more to do with Fiero's. Long story short, she's my ex-wife.<P>  Peace, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000002||wkayl||11-21-2000||08:31 AM||||In 1986 the company I work for had a contest nationwide to give away Fiero GTs. Our local company decided to give one away in our area. It was a 1986 ½ GT and it was RED. It was shown at local events, but was towed on a dolly. The only thing we were allowed to do with it was start it and listen to that great exhaust. All of us just looked at and thought "If only we could take it for a spin". <P>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<P>I never thought of it much after that until my son turned 16 in 98 and wanted a car. He wanted a Mustang or Comaro. Then he saw a Fiero. I thought, ok lets think about this: 2 seater (no pile of friends ridding around), 4 cylinder (not over powered), good gas mileage (easy on pocketbook), and high console (guy on guy side and girl on girl side). OK!! We picked up a 1984 SE, one owner car from a retired schoolteacher. I don't think she ever had it over 60 miles an hr. in its 110,000 miles. I came to that conclusion when a month later, I drove it 60 miles on the interstate at 75 miles an hour and made a rod rattle. After an overhaul that cost as much as the car (and I did the work myself) he now has a great car. I told him I was going to get one also, but mine would be a GT. <P>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<P>I was talking with my brother about my plans and he said his mother-in-law had a Fiero. Her husband had passed away 4 years before and wasn't driven since then. I asked to take a look at it. When he opened the garage door, there staring at me was a 1986 ½ Fiero GT with 75,000 miles. It was RED. Without driving it, without hearing it run, but only by sitting in it I said, "I'll take it". It took me 9 months (I kinda know what women now go through now) to scrape enough together to pay for it. I won't tell you how much I paid for it because stealing is against the law. It was a surprise to my wife when I drove it up in front of the house all paid for including taxes and insurance (and I'm still married). 8 days after getting my Fiero I took a 1,600 mile trip. To get ready for the trip all I had to do was change the oil, replace the alternator belt, wash and wax. <P>To read more on this go to my bio on f\Fierodave's web site at:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/cgi-bin/fiero/postings.cgi?action=reply&forum=3.+General+Fiero+Chat&number=1&topic=004458.cgi&TopicSubject=Your+first+Fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/cgi-bin/fiero/postings.cgi?action=reply&forum=3.+General+Fiero+Chat&number=1&topic=004458.cgi&TopicSubject=Your+first+Fiero</A> <BR>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick's Dad||11-21-2000||09:37 AM||||In 1983, I was designing my dream car. I had it from the A-pillar back, and even put it into a couple of drawings in art class. It was a rear engine SBC. I could never get the nose right, though....<P>During and shortly after Fiero production, I had my first three cars; a 1973 I6 Camaro (decked a Mercedes with it), a 1972 Camaro LT (Got decked by a motorcycle), and a 1979 280ZX (Got stolen. If it hadn't, it would have rotted out from under me....).<P>So, in 1989, I was briefly carless. I wasn't sure what I wanted, but I happened into the local Chevy dealer on my bike (bicycle. I used to ride hundreds of miles a year.). After talking to a sales girl (not by accident), I saw my first Fiero. I had probably seen them before, but I actually paid attention to this one. It was white, with a broad red stripe from the middle of the hood and up on the roof (not on the decklid), and red stripes on the sides that gathered from the front wheel well back to the rear quarters. I sat in it, I drove it, and the rest is history. I loved my car, but didn't know that much about it. I certainly didn't have this support group. Two years later, I had lost the rear calipers, the clutch, and the computer, all at once. I didn't have $1600.00 to fix it all, but, of course I could qualify for credit. I searched for three months to find my Formula, then (sob) gave my 2M4 to the Kidney Foundation, and took a deduction on my income tax. I sat in her the night before she left. It was not a happy time....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>This should work until my site comes online....   <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.rshuman/files/Tmh.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||msweldon||11-21-2000||10:10 AM||||1985 fiero gt, 4spd auto, blk w/ gray int., 14" alloys.  Purchased for 2500$ when I was 17 in 1991 at the Fairburn Rd. Amoco from the owner.  Fell in love with at first sight, got my dad hooked on them, and we've owned 4 since.<P>For a period of 3 years he and I drove "identical" 85gt's.  I sold mine for an 87gt fastback 5spd and his was hit head on by a bmw 1200cc bike doing 104mph and my dad doing 45mph, uugghhh, it was nasty.... He then purchased an 86.5 gt fastback.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||11-21-2000||11:32 AM||||had a '74 vette and '84 blazer.got both in '95. i moved to an area i was not too certain of and put the vette in storage. had about 6 mo. payments on it and when i paid it off i was looking around for some thing else. i had always liked fieros and had been seeing one on a local lot. $3000, stayed on the lot a long time and went on sale about the time i paid the vette off. $1995, about the same color as my chevy. it's my daily driver now. <BR> moved and got my chevy back. now they're big bruh and lil bruh. got a couple more since.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||11-21-2000||11:40 AM||||My first was a white 85 GT, purchased new. Had a 4-speed and all options except defog and power mirrors. Traded in a badly "curbed" (no, I didn't do it) MG midget. Kept it until 88, when I traded it in on a Firebird Formula -two weeks before they announced the Fiero's demise. <P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||11-21-2000||01:16 PM||||I bought my first Fiero in April 1990.  I drove by a dealer in my '82 Honda wagon and saw a beautiful black '88 GT.  I had been lusting over Fieros since '86 and figured I would stop and take it for a spin.  I was in love!  Of course just about anything would have ben an improvement over the Honda!<P>They let me take it home for the night to get a better feel for it.  That did the trick.....the next day I went in to buy it.  I paid a lot for it, but it was worth it.  It was $8995, but it only had a little over 20K miles on it.<P>I drove that Fiero everywhere and in less than 5 years it had 114K miles. It was in storage for almost 2 years while I was in the Navy, so I was averaging over 30K miles a year.  Sadly it was totalled in an accident in Dec. 1995.  That was a dark day for me.....I had planned on keeping it forever.  <P>Since then I have owned about 3 dozen Fieros.  I haven't found one that "fits" like my first one though......maybe that's why I keep buying and selling them.||||reg||10
Z||000008||jlhuber||11-21-2000||03:41 PM||||My first Fiero was purchased in early March 1998.  It was a '86 gt with an automatic and t-tops  by C&C.  The color was bright red with silver lower and light grey interior.  It had 64,000 miles on it when I bought.  The guy selling went to the same high school as I, and he lived just down the road from me too.  I had seen the car around town a few times on the *nice* days and then luckily, when I was in the market for a new car, I found that it was for sale in the local autotrader magazine.  Went to test drive it, fell in love the moment I sat down, paid the $5500 the next day.  That was my first Fiero.  Every day that goes by, I regret selling her.  I'm looking to replace her with another one though.  Ah well, time to stop this reminiscing and get back to work.  ...work!?...ha!<P>                      --Jess||||reg||14
Z||000009||PAUL88GT||11-21-2000||06:07 PM||||Bought an 86 coupe at a car auction for $325 three years ago.  Drove it home. The car did not look good, but it was mechanically solid. Loved the way it handled. Bought my 88GT and sold the coupe for a nice profit. I am now on the look-out for a good used Formula.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Tigger||11-21-2000||10:25 PM||||I bought my first Fiero in June '99.<BR>1984 Fiero SE, 4 spd. brown-biege interior<P>Day I picked it up & a year later and now:<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/before1.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/finishstage9.gif">     <P>I give a lot of credit to my Father Bruce, for sharing his experience with cars, and all that I've learned working with him through many years which help me build this car.  I also would like to give credit to my Uncle John, for all of his helpful advise and spraying the car's awesome finish.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-21-2000).]||||reg||7
